Let p be a prime number. Equip each of the following fields with its canonical valuation (the unique extension of the p-adic valuation on Q_p in cases (a)–(c), and the t-adic valuation in cases (d)–(e)):

(a) Q_p(ζ_{p^∞}) := ⋃_{n≥1} Q_p(ζ_{p^n}), where ζ_{p^n} is a primitive p^n-th root of unity;
(b) Q_p(p^{1/p^∞}) := ⋃_{n≥1} Q_p(p^{1/p^n}), where the p^{1/p^n} form a compatible system of p^n-th roots of p;
(c) Q_p^{ab}, the maximal abelian extension of Q_p inside a fixed algebraic closure;
(d) F_p((t))^{1/p^∞} := ⋃_{n≥0} F_p((t))^{1/p^n}, the perfect hull of F_p((t));
(e) F_p^{ac}((t))^{1/p^∞}, the perfect hull of F_p^{ac}((t)), where F_p^{ac} denotes an algebraic closure of F_p.

Let L_val denote the first-order language of valued rings (the language of rings together with a binary predicate for the valuation divisibility relation), and let L_val(t) denote L_val enlarged by a constant symbol naming t.

Determine whether the following theories are decidable:
(1) the L_val-theory of Q_p(ζ_{p^∞});
(2) the L_val-theory of Q_p(p^{1/p^∞});
(3) the L_val-theory of Q_p^{ab};
(4) the L_val-theory and the L_val(t)-theory of F_p((t))^{1/p^∞};
(5) the L_val-theory and the L_val(t)-theory of F_p^{ac}((t))^{1/p^∞}.

